, "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_15", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_09", "role": "support"}], "narrative": "303 and 1293 are both low-degree (4 events each) and share no neighbours; they are linked only through very high-degree hubs (1583, 28) when time is ignored. Under the 600-unit relay rule no time-respecting route exists, and none exists even with unlimited or 5000-unit waiting, because 303's activity is confined to the late part of the window while 1293's incoming contacts occur mostly early. The static connection and hub popularity are therefore confounds, not evidence of a cascade."}, "raw_final_response": "**Conclusion:** No time-respecting cascade exists.\n\nKey findings:\n- With the 600-unit relay constraint, 303 reaches only 10 nodes and never 1293 (step_01); zero paths enumerated (step_10).\n- Even with *no* wait limit, 303's temporal forward-set (85 nodes) excludes 1293 (step_02); relaxing to 5000 units also fails (step_12).\n- Confounder controls: a static (time-ignoring) route clearly exists via hubs \u2014 303\u21921583 and 28/1438\u21921293, with 1583 having 294 partners and 28 having 428 partners and 600 events (step_04, step_13) \u2014 yet that popularity does not yield an ordered path. Node 28 itself is not even reachable from 303 under the constraint (step_11). Timing explains why: 303's four events all fall in the late bins (\u226547496) while 1293's incoming activity is concentrated early (28296\u201347495) (step_14, step_15) \u2014 the target's contacts largely precede the source's activity, so ordering is wrong.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_dc3dfaa5\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [303, 1293],\n  \"window\": [28296, 66695],\n  \"confidence\": 0.91,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"303 and 1293 are both low-degree (4 events each) and share no neighbours; they are linked only through very high-degree hubs (1583, 28) when time is ignored. Under the 600-unit relay rule no time-respecting route exists, and none exists even with unlimited or 5000-unit waiting, because 303's activity is confined to the late part of the window while 1293's incoming contacts occur mostly early. The static connection and hub popularity are therefore confounds, not evidence of a cascade.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 51363, "total_output_tokens": 3640, "total_tokens": 55003, "total_latency_ms": 59288, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}